    </exercise>

<exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why do the stars <em>twinkle</em> in a night sky? How about the same stars if seen from international space station?
      </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What makes setting sun appear red?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        scattering of light from the atmospheric air molecules.
      </p>
    </answer>
    </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why do we see objects as white? 
      </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>
  
<exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The American flag appears <fillin characters="10" /> 
        when viewed in blue light.
         <ol marker="a">
          <li>red and blue.</li>
          <li>blue and black</li> 
            <li>all blue.</li> 
              <li>red, white, and blue.</li> 
           </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
      White is a combination of all the colors, and black is the absense of all colors. 
      When viewed in the blue light only blue light reflects. The blue parts are not affected, the red parts are black now, 
      and the white parts are blue now.
      </p>
    </hint>
    </exercise>


